<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!--
  PreTeXt AI-badge problem document template.

  Intended use:
  1. Copy this file into a course PreTeXt source tree.
  2. Make the badge SVGs available in the path used by the <image source="..."> elements.
  3. Optionally add templates/pretext/ai-badge-problem-document.css as an extra HTML stylesheet.
  4. Replace MATH 4362, Example Problem Set: Root-Finding and Error, problem text, and point values.
-->
<pretext>
  <article xml:id="ai-badge-problem-document-template">
    <title>Example Problem Set: Root-Finding and Error</title>
    <subtitle>MATH 4362 · AI-use badge problem document</subtitle>

    <frontmatter>
      <author>
        <personname>Nicholas S. Moore</personname>
        <department>Department of Mathematics</department>
        <institution>West Texas A&amp;M University</institution>
      </author>
    </frontmatter>

    <section xml:id="ai-badge-policy-guide">
      <title>How to read the AI-use badges</title>
      <p xml:id="ai-badge-intro" custom-class="ai-badge-intro">
        Each activity below has its own AI-use policy. The badge gives the quick visual cue,
        but the written note below it is the authoritative instruction. When AI is permitted,
        students are still responsible for checking, understanding, and explaining their
        submitted work.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section xml:id="example-problems-with-ai-badges">
      <title>Example Problems</title>

      <paragraphs xml:id="problem-ai-not-permitted" custom-class="ai-problem-card">
        <title>Problem 1. Bisection by hand <nbsp/> <em>10 points</em></title>
        <image source="https://nsmoore57.github.io/ai-course-policy-badges/badges/ai-not-permitted.svg" width="35%" custom-class="ai-policy-badge">
          <description>AI Not Permitted. Students must complete the marked work without AI assistance.</description>
        </image>
        <p custom-class="ai-policy-note">
          <alert>AI Use: AI Not Permitted.</alert>
            Complete this problem without AI assistance. Use this for unaided understanding,
            foundational skills, or individual reasoning.
        </p>
        <p>Let <m>f(x)=x^3-x-2</m>. Starting with the interval <m>[1,2]</m>, perform four iterations of the bisection method.</p>
        <ol marker="a">
          <li><p>Record the midpoint, function value sign, and retained interval at each step.</p></li>
          <li><p>Give the final midpoint approximation after four iterations.</p></li>
          <li><p>State a valid upper bound for the absolute error.</p></li>
        </ol>
      </paragraphs>

      <paragraphs xml:id="problem-ai-debugging-only" custom-class="ai-problem-card">
        <title>Problem 2. Debugging a Newton iteration <nbsp/> <em>15 points</em></title>
        <image source="https://nsmoore57.github.io/ai-course-policy-badges/badges/ai-debugging-only.svg" width="35%" custom-class="ai-policy-badge">
          <description>AI Debugging Only. Students may use AI to understand errors, diagnose problems, and ask guiding questions, but not to generate a full solution.</description>
        </image>
        <p custom-class="ai-policy-note">
          <alert>AI Use: AI Debugging Only.</alert>
            You may use AI to interpret error messages, diagnose bugs, or ask guiding questions.
            Do not ask AI to write the full solution or complete the analysis for you.
        </p>
        <p>The following Python function is intended to implement Newton&apos;s method, but it contains at least two bugs.</p>
        <program language="python">
          <code>def newton(f, df, x0, tol=1e-8, max_iter=20):
    x = x0
    for k in range(max_iter):
        x_next = x - f(x) / df
        if abs(x_next - x0) &lt; tol:
            return x_next
        x = x_next
    return x</code>
        </program>
        <ol marker="a">
          <li><p>Identify and fix the bugs.</p></li>
          <li><p>Test the corrected function on <m>f(x)=x^2-2</m>.</p></li>
          <li><p>Briefly explain why your stopping condition is appropriate.</p></li>
        </ol>
      </paragraphs>

      <paragraphs xml:id="problem-ai-permitted-disclosure" custom-class="ai-problem-card">
        <title>Problem 3. Method comparison reflection <nbsp/> <em>15 points</em></title>
        <image source="https://nsmoore57.github.io/ai-course-policy-badges/badges/ai-permitted-disclosure.svg" width="45%" custom-class="ai-policy-badge">
          <description>AI Permitted with Disclosure. Students may use AI as a tutor, assistant, brainstorming partner, or reviewer, but must disclose its use and understand submitted work.</description>
        </image>
        <p custom-class="ai-policy-note">
          <alert>AI Use: AI Permitted with Disclosure.</alert>
            You may use AI as a tutor, coding assistant, brainstorming partner, or reviewer.
            Disclose how you used it, and be prepared to explain every part of your submission.
        </p>
        <p>Compare bisection and Newton&apos;s method for solving nonlinear equations.</p>
        <ol marker="a">
          <li><p>Describe one advantage and one limitation of each method.</p></li>
          <li><p>Give an example of a situation where bisection is preferable.</p></li>
          <li><p>If you used AI, include a short disclosure note summarizing what you asked and how you checked the response.</p></li>
        </ol>
      </paragraphs>
    </section>
  </article>
</pretext>
